WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.225
Lesson one point four.

00:00:02.225 --> 00:00:04.735
Tokens, approvals and allowances.

00:00:04.735 --> 00:00:06.739
By the end, you'll be able to read an

00:00:06.739 --> 00:00:09.640
approval request and know exactly what you're allowing.

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:15.162
You've already met approvals twice: the four actions in

00:00:15.162 --> 00:00:19.910
Lesson 0.7, and the before-signing checklist in Lesson 1.0.

00:00:19.910 --> 00:00:22.292
This lesson goes one layer deeper, into

00:00:22.292 --> 00:00:24.317
what a token actually is, and the

00:00:24.317 --> 00:00:27.355
specific trick that makes some approvals invisible.

00:00:28.655 --> 00:00:29.945
Here's the plan.

00:00:29.945 --> 00:00:34.690
First, what tokens actually are, fungible, non-fungible, and wrapped.

00:00:34.690 --> 00:00:38.575
Second, the specific danger of gasless signature approvals.

00:00:38.575 --> 00:00:39.769
Third, how to know a

00:00:39.769 --> 00:00:41.320
token is the real one.

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:43.468
And finally, a real worked example

00:00:43.468 --> 00:00:45.555
reading an actual approval prompt.

00:00:46.855 --> 00:00:48.250
What tokens actually are.

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:55.105
Most tokens on Ethereum-compatible chains are ERC-20, fungible,

00:00:55.105 --> 00:00:58.838
meaning any one is identical to any other,

00:00:58.838 --> 00:01:02.397
like USDC or ETH. NFTs, ERC-721 or 1155,

00:01:02.397 --> 00:01:06.997
are non-fungible, each one unique, and can represent

00:01:06.997 --> 00:01:11.290
things like a concentrated liquidity position.

00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:14.010
Wrapped tokens, like WETH, represent a

00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:17.100
claim on something else, held elsewhere.

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:21.150
LP tokens are a receipt for liquidity you've provided.

00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:23.651
And lending receipts represent a claim on

00:01:23.651 --> 00:01:26.330
whatever you deposited into a lending market.

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:30.265
Notice what wrapped and receipt tokens have

00:01:30.265 --> 00:01:32.301
in common: they're all a claim, a

00:01:32.301 --> 00:01:34.756
piece of paper standing in for something

00:01:34.756 --> 00:01:37.930
held elsewhere, not the underlying asset itself.

00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:41.848
That distinction matters constantly from here on, especially

00:01:41.848 --> 00:01:45.060
once Module 2 covers liquidity positions in depth.

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:49.800
Take WETH as the clearest example.

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:53.942
One WETH is designed to always be redeemable for

00:01:53.942 --> 00:01:59.652
exactly one ETH, backed one-to-one inside the wrapping contract.

00:01:59.652 --> 00:02:03.558
The wrapping exists purely for compatibility, since some

00:02:03.558 --> 00:02:07.147
older token standards can't handle ETH directly.

00:02:07.147 --> 00:02:10.188
Convenient, but it's still a separate contract you're now

00:02:10.188 --> 00:02:12.547
trusting, on top of the one you started with.

00:02:13.847 --> 00:02:15.222
The invisible approval.

00:02:16.522 --> 00:02:19.622
Quick recap, since you've seen the basics twice already.

00:02:19.622 --> 00:02:21.967
To let a protocol move your tokens, you

00:02:21.967 --> 00:02:24.840
approve a spender for an allowance; the protocol

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:26.957
then pulls tokens when you act.

00:02:26.957 --> 00:02:30.354
Unlimited approvals are convenient, but they let that

00:02:30.354 --> 00:02:32.933
contract move all of that token, now and

00:02:32.933 --> 00:02:34.632
at any point in the future.

00:02:35.932 --> 00:02:38.211
Under the hood, this is just two function

00:02:38.211 --> 00:02:40.952
calls, written into the token's own standard.

00:02:40.952 --> 00:02:43.697
Approve, which you call, grants the allowance.

00:02:43.697 --> 00:02:47.746
Transfer-from, which the spender calls later, actually moves the

00:02:47.746 --> 00:02:51.297
tokens, using exactly the allowance you granted, no more.

00:02:52.597 --> 00:02:54.817
Here's what's genuinely new in this

00:02:54.817 --> 00:02:58.077
lesson: signature approvals, Permit and Permit2.

00:02:58.077 --> 00:03:00.230
Instead of an on-chain transaction,

00:03:00.230 --> 00:03:02.324
you sign an off-chain message.

00:03:02.324 --> 00:03:04.529
That message costs no gas at all.

00:03:04.529 --> 00:03:07.119
With no gas, and no separate confirmation

00:03:07.119 --> 00:03:09.956
screen slowing you down, it's remarkably easy

00:03:09.956 --> 00:03:12.229
to sign without really noticing.

00:03:12.229 --> 00:03:14.647
And once signed, the allowance it grants

00:03:14.647 --> 00:03:16.534
is exactly as real as any other.

00:03:17.834 --> 00:03:19.809
Here's that gap, made concrete.

00:03:19.809 --> 00:03:23.074
A normal on-chain approval costs real gas, a few

00:03:23.074 --> 00:03:27.639
dollars, a genuine transaction with its own confirmation screen.

00:03:27.639 --> 00:03:30.712
A Permit-style signature approval costs nothing at

00:03:30.712 --> 00:03:32.941
all, no fee, no separate friction to

00:03:32.941 --> 00:03:35.229
slow you down and make you think.

00:03:35.229 --> 00:03:38.434
That zero is exactly why drainers prefer this method.

00:03:39.734 --> 00:03:41.473
The defence is the same habit

00:03:41.473 --> 00:03:43.859
this whole module has been building.

00:03:43.859 --> 00:03:47.874
Read every prompt, transaction or signature, with equal care.

00:03:47.874 --> 00:03:50.728
A wallet that shows 'Signature Request' instead

00:03:50.728 --> 00:03:53.704
of 'Transaction' isn't asking for less trust.

00:03:53.704 --> 00:03:55.489
If anything, it deserves more.

00:03:56.789 --> 00:03:58.744
Here's a detail worth sitting with.

00:03:58.744 --> 00:04:01.432
An unlimited approval doesn't expire when you

00:04:01.432 --> 00:04:03.301
close the tab, or even when you

00:04:03.301 --> 00:04:05.354
forget about the app entirely.

00:04:05.354 --> 00:04:08.306
It sits there, live, for months or years, until

00:04:08.306 --> 00:04:11.374
you either revoke it or the contract uses it.

00:04:11.374 --> 00:04:13.234
Time doesn't make it safer.

00:04:13.234 --> 00:04:15.084
It just makes it easier to forget.

00:04:16.384 --> 00:04:17.459
Real, or fake?

00:04:18.759 --> 00:04:20.599
Here's a fact worth sitting with.

00:04:20.599 --> 00:04:24.406
Anyone, literally anyone, can create a token and name

00:04:24.406 --> 00:04:28.019
it 'USDC', with the exact same ticker and logo.

00:04:28.019 --> 00:04:31.672
Only one thing actually identifies a token: its contract

00:04:31.672 --> 00:04:35.838
address, verified from an authoritative source, never typed from

00:04:35.838 --> 00:04:39.363
memory and never trusted just because the name matches.

00:04:40.663 --> 00:04:43.546
Here's how a fake-token scam actually plays out,

00:04:43.546 --> 00:04:45.788
so you recognise the shape of it.

00:04:45.788 --> 00:04:48.968
A token appears, using a familiar name, ticker

00:04:48.968 --> 00:04:53.328
and logo, often airdropped directly into wallets, uninvited.

00:04:53.328 --> 00:04:56.137
It shows up trading on a real-looking exchange

00:04:56.137 --> 00:04:58.888
listing, sometimes briefly on a real one.

00:04:58.888 --> 00:05:00.772
You approve or swap it, checking

00:05:00.772 --> 00:05:03.184
the name, not the underlying address.

00:05:03.184 --> 00:05:05.462
And the contract does whatever its creator

00:05:05.462 --> 00:05:07.793
actually wrote, which might be nothing like

00:05:07.793 --> 00:05:09.224
what a real token would do.

00:05:10.524 --> 00:05:13.294
Some fake tokens even have a real liquidity

00:05:13.294 --> 00:05:15.875
pool set up, letting them show an actual

00:05:15.875 --> 00:05:18.708
price and trade normally, right up until the

00:05:18.708 --> 00:05:21.904
moment the creator empties that pool entirely.

00:05:21.904 --> 00:05:24.759
A price chart existing doesn't make a token

00:05:24.759 --> 00:05:27.744
real; only the verified contract address does.

00:05:29.044 --> 00:05:31.009
Here's the practical difference.

00:05:31.009 --> 00:05:33.819
A real USDC approval shows a contract

00:05:33.819 --> 00:05:36.926
address matching the one published by the

00:05:36.926 --> 00:05:40.184
issuer, checkable on any block explorer.

00:05:40.184 --> 00:05:43.350
A fake shows a different address entirely, dressed up

00:05:43.350 --> 00:05:46.282
with an identical name and symbol, banking on you

00:05:46.282 --> 00:05:49.272
never checking the one field that actually matters.

00:05:50.572 --> 00:05:55.292
NFT approvals work a little differently, worth knowing separately.

00:05:55.292 --> 00:05:58.037
Instead of an amount, 'set approval for

00:05:58.037 --> 00:06:00.851
all' grants a spender control over every

00:06:00.851 --> 00:06:03.184
single NFT you own in that entire

00:06:03.184 --> 00:06:06.092
collection, all at once, not just one.

00:06:06.092 --> 00:06:09.023
That's an even bigger blast radius than a typical

00:06:09.023 --> 00:06:12.599
token allowance, worth double-checking every time it appears.

00:06:13.899 --> 00:06:16.690
Fake tokens and drainer scams both get

00:06:16.690 --> 00:06:19.052
a full lesson of their own soon,

00:06:19.052 --> 00:06:22.214
Lesson 1.6, scam defence, in real depth.

00:06:22.214 --> 00:06:25.804
For now, contract-address verification and reading every prompt

00:06:25.804 --> 00:06:27.879
are enough to catch nearly all of it.

00:06:29.179 --> 00:06:30.244
A worked example.

00:06:31.544 --> 00:06:33.734
Here's the prompt, word for word.

00:06:33.734 --> 00:06:38.065
'Allow 0x3fC9…a1B2 to spend your USDC.

00:06:38.065 --> 00:06:43.029
Amount: Unlimited.' Four checks, in order.

00:06:43.029 --> 00:06:46.839
One: is that address actually the protocol's official router?

00:06:46.839 --> 00:06:49.679
Check the docs or an explorer, never assume.

00:06:49.679 --> 00:06:51.999
Two: do you actually need unlimited?

00:06:51.999 --> 00:06:53.547
Edit it down to the exact

00:06:53.547 --> 00:06:55.454
amount of this one deposit.

00:06:55.454 --> 00:06:59.709
Three: is this USDC actually the real contract?

00:06:59.709 --> 00:07:02.431
And four: once you're finished with this app,

00:07:02.431 --> 00:07:05.390
revoke the approval with a tool like revoke.cash,

00:07:05.390 --> 00:07:06.929
if you won't be back soon.

00:07:08.229 --> 00:07:10.762
Notice this is the exact same shape

00:07:10.762 --> 00:07:14.439
as the before-signing checklist from Lesson 1.0.

00:07:14.439 --> 00:07:17.635
It's just applied specifically to tokens now: confirm

00:07:17.635 --> 00:07:20.594
the spender, limit the amount, verify what you're

00:07:20.594 --> 00:07:23.139
actually approving, and clean up afterward.

00:07:24.439 --> 00:07:27.233
This is the third time this program

00:07:27.233 --> 00:07:30.416
has walked through a revoke habit, after

00:07:30.416 --> 00:07:32.734
Lesson 0.7 and Lesson 1.0.

00:07:32.734 --> 00:07:34.869
That repetition is deliberate.

00:07:34.869 --> 00:07:37.930
It's one of the highest-value five-minute habits in

00:07:37.930 --> 00:07:40.461
this entire program, and the only one that

00:07:40.461 --> 00:07:43.639
actually undoes standing risk you've already taken on.

00:07:44.939 --> 00:07:46.119
Checklist and quiz.

00:07:47.419 --> 00:07:49.954
One balancing note before the checklist.

00:07:49.954 --> 00:07:53.198
Unlimited approvals aren't purely a trap; they exist

00:07:53.198 --> 00:07:55.831
for real convenience, so you don't need to

00:07:55.831 --> 00:07:59.198
approve again for every single future interaction with

00:07:59.198 --> 00:08:01.334
a protocol you use constantly.

00:08:01.334 --> 00:08:04.602
The judgment call is deciding when that convenience is

00:08:04.602 --> 00:08:07.394
actually worth the exposure, and when it isn't.

00:08:08.694 --> 00:08:10.589
Here's this lesson's checklist.

00:08:10.589 --> 00:08:14.464
The spender is verified against official docs, not assumed.

00:08:14.464 --> 00:08:17.584
The allowance is limited to what's actually needed.

00:08:17.584 --> 00:08:21.259
Signature requests are read as carefully as transactions.

00:08:21.259 --> 00:08:23.799
And approvals are reviewed and revoked on

00:08:23.799 --> 00:08:26.399
a regular schedule, not left to accumulate.

00:08:27.699 --> 00:08:28.909
Question one.

00:08:28.909 --> 00:08:31.474
Why is a gasless 'Permit' signature dangerous?

00:08:35.894 --> 00:08:38.954
The answer: it can grant a token allowance without

00:08:38.954 --> 00:08:42.434
a transaction, so it's easy to sign without noticing.

00:08:42.434 --> 00:08:44.964
Drainers use exactly this to take tokens.

00:08:46.264 --> 00:08:47.509
Question two.

00:08:47.509 --> 00:08:48.928
How do you actually know a

00:08:48.928 --> 00:08:50.084
token is the real one?

00:08:54.504 --> 00:08:58.014
The answer: by its contract address, from an authoritative

00:08:58.014 --> 00:09:00.394
source, never by its name or its ticker.

00:09:01.694 --> 00:09:02.964
Question three.

00:09:02.964 --> 00:09:04.609
When should you revoke an approval?

00:09:09.029 --> 00:09:11.422
The answer: when you no longer use that

00:09:11.422 --> 00:09:14.174
protocol, or on a regular schedule either way.

00:09:15.474 --> 00:09:16.729
Question four.

00:09:16.729 --> 00:09:19.616
True or false: an NFT and a fungible

00:09:19.616 --> 00:09:23.439
token can both represent a DeFi position. [[pause

00:09:27.739 --> 00:09:29.234
4]] The answer:

00:09:29.234 --> 00:09:32.857
true. A concentrated liquidity position, for instance,

00:09:32.857 --> 00:09:35.821
is commonly represented as an NFT, precisely

00:09:35.821 --> 00:09:37.599
because each one is unique.

00:09:38.899 --> 00:09:40.214
Question five.

00:09:40.214 --> 00:09:41.488
A wrapped token and a

00:09:41.488 --> 00:09:43.574
lending receipt both represent what?

00:09:47.994 --> 00:09:50.258
The answer: a claim on something else,

00:09:50.258 --> 00:09:53.044
held elsewhere, not the underlying asset itself.

00:09:54.344 --> 00:09:57.154
Here's the whole lesson, recapped as one loop.

00:09:57.154 --> 00:09:59.299
Know what kind of token you're holding.

00:09:59.299 --> 00:10:01.604
Verify the spender before approving.

00:10:01.604 --> 00:10:03.679
Limit the allowance to what you need.

00:10:03.679 --> 00:10:05.753
Read a signature request exactly

00:10:05.753 --> 00:10:07.939
as carefully as a transaction.

00:10:07.939 --> 00:10:11.134
Verify by contract address, never by name.

00:10:11.134 --> 00:10:12.779
And revoke on a schedule.

00:10:12.779 --> 00:10:14.907
Do those six things, and an approval

00:10:14.907 --> 00:10:17.034
can never quietly outlive its reason.

00:10:18.334 --> 00:10:22.025
This is education, not financial advice, and every dollar

00:10:22.025 --> 00:10:26.079
figure and contract address in this lesson is illustrative.

00:10:26.079 --> 00:10:28.761
Nobody from this program will ever ask for

00:10:28.761 --> 00:10:31.818
your seed phrase, private keys or account access.

00:10:33.117 --> 00:10:37.082
That's tokens, approvals and allowances: what tokens actually

00:10:37.082 --> 00:10:40.088
are, why signature approvals are easy to miss,

00:10:40.088 --> 00:10:42.307
and how to verify what's real.

00:10:42.307 --> 00:10:44.178
Next, Lesson one point five:

00:10:44.178 --> 00:10:46.177
stablecoins and how they break.
